Your hypothesis is structurally correct, and the code contains a concrete, unconditional latch that makes it permanent. The decisive line is not in the driver but in `tcp_sendcommon()`: **`conn->rcv_adv` is committed when the TCP header is built, before the frame is handed to the driver, and it is never rolled back if the driver drops the frame.** Combined with the fact that no TCP timer is armed when `tx_unacked == 0` (your `uack=0`), a single dropped window-update ACK means TCP will never generate another one. Recovery only happens when the peer's persist/retransmit timer fires and an inbound segment forces a fresh ACK — which matches your 10-28 s dead stretch exactly.

