## 2. Does `netdev_txnotify_dev` reach the driver? Yes — and it is fully synchronous. The problem is what happens after.

### `netdev_txnotify_dev()` — nuttx/net/netdev/netdev_txnotify.c:112-124
```c
void netdev_txnotify_dev(FAR struct net_driver_s *dev, uint32_t polltype)
{
  if (dev != NULL && dev->d_txavail != NULL)
    {
      dev->d_polltype |= polltype;
      dev->d_txavail(dev);
    }
}
```

### The driver is a lowerhalf driver
`contest2026_264_VelaSightsuixingAIzhinengyanjing/board/beken/chips/bk7258/bk7258_wifi.c:2866`:
```c
  ret = netdev_lower_register(&g_wifi.lower, NET_LL_IEEE80211);
```
It does **not** implement `d_txavail` itself. `netdev_upperhalf.c:1512` installs it:
```c
  dev->netdev.d_txavail = netdev_upper_txavail;
```
and `netdev_upper_txavail` (netdev_upperhalf.c:890-894) calls `netdev_upper_txavail_work()` **inline, in the caller's context** — no work queue, no deferral. So the notification does reach the poll machinery.

`g_netdev_ops` (bk7258_wifi.c:2754-2762) provides only `.ifup`, `.ifdown`, `.transmit`, `.receive`, `.ioctl`. **No `.reclaim`.** That matters below.

### A pure ACK *can* be produced and transmitted
`netdev_upper_tx()` (netdev_upperhalf.c:344-366) → `devif_poll(dev, netdev_upper_txpoll)`. Because the lowerhalf is IOB-only (`dev->d_buf == NULL`), `devif_poll()` takes the `devif_iob_poll()` branch (devif_poll.c:1141-1147), which runs `devif_poll_connections()` → `case TCP_POLL:` → `devif_poll_tcp_connections()` (devif_poll.c:655-677) → `tcp_poll()` → `tcp_callback(dev, conn, TCP_POLL)` → `tcp_appsend()`. With no app data, `tcp_appsend()` still ORs in `TCP_SNDACK` from `tcp_should_send_recvwindow()` and `tcp_rexmit()` emits `tcp_send(dev, conn, TCP_ACK, hdrlen)`, setting `dev->d_len` to the 40-byte header. `devif_poll_callback()` (devif_poll.c:1082-1090) then returns true because `d_len > 0`, and `netdev_upper_txpoll()` calls `lower->ops->transmit()`. So the path is **not** structurally blocked by "no application data"; there is no send-queue-empty bailout.

### Where the request *is* silently dropped — four places

**(a) The `txing` re-entrancy guard — netdev_upperhalf.c:382-400 (decisive line 389)**
```c
static void netdev_upper_txavail_work(FAR struct netdev_upperhalf_s *upper)
{
  FAR struct net_driver_s *dev = &upper->lower->netdev;

  netdev_lock(dev);
  if (IFF_IS_UP(dev->d_flags) && !upper->txing)
    {
      DEBUGASSERT(dev->d_buf == NULL); /* Make sure: IOB only. */
      upper->txing = true;
      while (netdev_upper_can_tx(upper) &&
             netdev_upper_tx(dev) == NETDEV_TX_CONTINUE);
      upper->txing = false;
    }

  netdev_unlock(dev);
}
```
If `upper->txing` is already set (a txavail arriving from inside the poll, e.g. re-entered via the RX/txdone worker), the notification is **discarded with no rescheduling**. Same for `!IFF_IS_UP`.

**(b) TX quota exhausted with no `reclaim` — netdev_upperhalf.c:245-257**
```c
static inline bool netdev_upper_can_tx(FAR struct netdev_upperhalf_s *upper)
{
  int quota = netdev_lower_quota_load(lower, NETPKT_TX);
  if (quota <= 0 && lower->ops->reclaim)
    { lower->ops->reclaim(lower); quota = netdev_lower_quota_load(lower, NETPKT_TX); }
  return quota > 0;
}
```
`g_wifi.lower.quota[NETPKT_TX] = CONFIG_BK7258_WIFI_TX_SLOTS` = **8** (bk7258_wifi.c:2826; `.config:287`). With no `.reclaim`, when all 8 slots are in flight the `while` loop body never executes; the function returns having sent nothing and **without rescheduling**. Recovery depends entirely on `netdev_lower_txdone()` (netdev_upperhalf.c:1692-1701, which re-calls `netdev_upper_txavail_work`), driven from `wifi_notify_network()` (bk7258_wifi.c:1336-1346).

**(c) `wifi_transmit()` error → the ACK packet is destroyed, not retried — bk7258_wifi.c:1994-2081**
```c
  if (!g_wifi.carrier || !g_wifi.tx_gate ||
      g_wifi.role_state != WIFI_ROLE_ACTIVE ||
      g_wifi.active_role == WIFI_ROLE_NONE ||
      !bk7258_mailbox_link_ready())
    { nxmutex_unlock(&g_wifi.packet_lock); return -ENETDOWN; }
  ...
  if (tx == NULL)
    {
      printf("bk7258_wifi: AP TX slots exhausted\n");
      nxmutex_unlock(&g_wifi.packet_lock);
      return -EBUSY;
    }
```
Back in `netdev_upper_txpoll()` (netdev_upperhalf.c:312-324):
```c
  if (ret != OK)
    {
      NETDEV_TXERRORS(dev);
      nerr("ERROR: Transmit failed: %d\n", ret);
      netpkt_put(dev, pkt, NETPKT_TX);
      return ret;
    }
```
`netpkt_put()` just restores quota and re-attaches the IOB to `dev` (netdev_upperhalf.c:191-202); the negative return stops the poll, and `devif_iob_poll()` then does `netdev_iob_release(dev)` (devif_poll.c:1062). **The window-update ACK is freed. No retry is scheduled.** Note the "AP TX slots exhausted" printf is a concrete log line to grep for on your board — its presence during the stall would confirm this branch.

**(d) Work-queue coalescing — netdev_upperhalf.c:849-861**
The driver sets `g_wifi.lower.rxtype = NETDEV_RX_WORK` (bk7258_wifi.c:2828). `netdev_upper_queue_work()` does `if (work_available(work)) work_queue(...)`. Already-pending work is coalesced. This is mostly benign (the queued `netdev_upper_work` does RX then TX), but it does mean an RX-driven poll request can be merged into an earlier one.

### The latch that makes this permanent — nuttx/net/tcp/tcp_send.c:150-176

```c
      /* Update the TCP received window based on I/O buffer availability */

      uint32_t rcvseq = tcp_getsequence(conn->rcvseq);
      uint32_t recvwndo = tcp_get_recvwindow(dev, conn);

      /* Update the Receiver Window */

      conn->rcv_adv = rcvseq + recvwndo;
      ...
      tcp->wnd[0] = recvwndo >> 8;
      tcp->wnd[1] = recvwndo & 0xff;
```

`conn->rcv_adv` is committed here, in `tcp_sendcommon()`, at header-construction time — strictly *before* `netdev_upper_txpoll()` calls `lower->ops->transmit()`. So in cases (c) — and in any case where the frame is built but lost — the stack records "I advertised `rcvseq + win`" for a packet the peer never saw. On the next evaluation, `oldwin == win`, so `tcp_should_send_recvwindow()` hits `if (win <= oldwin) return false;` and **never re-advertises**.

Grepping every writer of `rcv_adv` in the tree confirms there is no repair path in ESTABLISHED:
- `tcp_send.c:165` (the commit above)
- `tcp_conn.c:1186`, `tcp_input.c:1541`, `tcp_input.c:1574` — connection setup only (SYN/SYN-SENT/SYN-RCVD)
- `sixlowpan_tcpsend.c:273` — not your path

And there is no timer to retry, which is exactly consistent with your `uack=0`. In `tcp_sendcommon()` (tcp_send.c:151-155):
```c
      if (work_available(&conn->work) && conn->tx_unacked != 0)
        {
          conn->timeout = false;
          tcp_update_retrantimer(conn, conn->rto);
        }
```
The retransmission timer is armed **only when `tx_unacked != 0`**. A pure ACK does not increment `tx_unacked`. With `uack=0` and `txsz=0` there is no armed `tcp_timer_expiry()` (tcp_timer.c:151-172, the only other `netdev_txnotify_dev(dev, TCP_POLL)` for an established conn), so nothing ever re-polls this connection. The connection is frozen until the server's persist timer produces a probe/retransmit, whose `tcp_input` → `tcp_appsend` → `tcp_send(..., TCP_ACK, ...)` rebuilds the header and re-advertises the (now large) window. That is your 10-28 s recovery, and it also explains why ICMP keeps flowing (ICMP uses `ICMP_POLL`, a different `d_polltype` bit and an independent trigger).

